<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sitka</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as New Archangel, Novo-Arkhangelsk</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sitka is a city on Baranof Island in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>. Founded as New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Archangel</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-baranov">Alexander Baranov</a> in 1804, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlingit">Tlingit</a> had destroyed an earlier Russian fort in 1802, it became the capital of Russian America in 1808. The transfer of Alaska to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> took place there in 1867, and it remained the territorial capital until 1906.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sitka.jpg" alt="Sitka" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gillfoto · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sitka_84_Elev_135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sitka lies on the west side of Baranof Island and the south half of Chichagof Island, in the Alexander Archipelago of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, in the Alaska Panhandle. Its name comes from the Tlingit Sheetʼká, a contraction of Shee Atʼiká, meaning people on the edge of Shee, Shee being the Tlingit name for Baranof Island. Mount Edgecumbe, a stratovolcano of about 3,200 feet, stands on Kruzof Island about 24 km to the west. Under Russian rule the settlement was called New Archangel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Russian explorers settled Old Sitka in 1799 and named it the Fort of Archangel Michael. Alexander Baranov, governor of Russian America, arrived under the auspices of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-american-company">Russian-American Company</a>, a trading company chartered by Emperor Paul I. In June 1802 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlingit">Tlingit</a> warriors destroyed the original settlement and killed many of the Russians, and only a few escaped. Baranov had to pay a ransom of 10,000 rubles to Captain Barber of the British ship Unicorn for the safe return of the surviving settlers.</p><p>The transfer ceremony for the Alaska Purchase took place at Sitka on 18 October 1867. The agreement to buy Alaska had been made in April of that year, and the price was 7.2 million dollars. According to the account, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which had lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> in 1856, wished to sell before British Canadians tried to conquer the territory, and Secretary of State William Seward had long wanted to buy it. </p><p>In 1880 Sitka, with 916 residents, was the largest community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>, of whom 540 were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlingit">Tlingit</a>, 219 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> and 157 White. By 1890 Juneau had overtaken it. The Alaska Native Brotherhood was founded in Sitka in 1912 to address racism against Alaska Native people, and by 1914 it had built its hall on Katlian Street, named after a Tlingit war chief of the early Russian period. Sitka was incorporated as a city in 1913.</p><p>In 1937 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> established the first seaplane base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> on Japonski Island, and in 1941 construction began on Fort Ray, an army garrison to protect it. The naval station was deactivated in June 1944. The Alaska Pulp Corporation, the first Japanese investment in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, began producing pulp in 1959 from the Tongass National Forest and closed in 1993.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sitka is a unified city-borough, formed by incorporation in 2000, and covers a total area of 4,811 square miles, much of it water. Sitka National Historical Park lies within it, and the Tongass National Forest, one of several protected areas around it, borders it. The O'Connell Bridge, built in 1972, joins the town to Japonski Island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sitka, Alaska” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In Alaska there are federally recognised tribes, among them the Central Council of the Tlingit &amp; Haida Indian Tribes, the <b>Sitka</b> Tribe of Alaska and the Yakutat Tlingit Tribe.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tlingit">Tlingit · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Sitka</b>, the Russian flag was lowered and the US flag raised, formally transferring Alaska, bought for 7.2 million dollars.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-18">18 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Company's chief managers ruled from Kodiak and, from 1808, from <b>New Archangel</b> (Sitka), built by Alexander Baranov after the Tlingit destroyed an earlier fort in 1802.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-america">Russian America</a></li></ul></section>
